mode).", 'created_at': '2026-07-22T15:58:13+00:00', 'path': 'staging/src/k8s.io/api/scheduling/v1alpha3/types.go', 'position': 4}, {'user': 'mm4tt', 'body': "NIT: Let's remove item != nil. We should be doing nil checks in `validateWorkloadItemTree`. ", 'created_at': '2026-07-22T15:59:56+00:00', 'path': 'staging/src/k8s.io/component-helpers/scheduling/schedulingv1/workloadbuilder/builder.go', 'position': 1}, {'user': 'mm4tt', 'body': "NIT: IMHO, user was a good name - because that was user provided config. I'd changed the name back.", 'created_at': '2026-07-22T16:00:49+00:00', 'path': 'staging/src/k8s.io/component-helpers/scheduling/schedulingv1/workloadbuilder/builder.go', 'position': 162}, {'user': 'mm4tt', 'body': 'These methods return []string, but we only use the result to check if len > 0.

I think this introduces unnecessary code. Let's keep it simple, as I suggested:

```
// validateItemInputUnion enforces the WorkloadInput leaf/composite union at
// build time. A composite node must not set any leaf building block, and a leaf
// node must not set any composite building block.
func validateItemInputUnion(item *WorkloadItem) error {
	in := item.Input

	if isComposite(item) {
		if in.Policy.PodGroupData != nil { return fmt.Errorf("composite workload item %q sets leaf building block: policy", item.Name) }
		if in.Constraints.PodGroupData != nil { return fmt.Errorf("composite workload item %q sets leaf building block: constraints", item.Name) }
		if in.DisruptionMode.PodGroupData != nil { return fmt.Errorf("composite workload item %q sets leaf building block: disruptionMode", item.Name) }
		if in.PreemptionPolicy.PodGroupData != nil { return fmt.Errorf("composite workload item %q sets leaf building block: preemptionPolicy", item.Name) }
		if len(in.ResourceClaims.PodGroupData) > 0 { return fmt.Errorf("composite workload item %q sets leaf building block: resourceClaims", item.Name) }
		return nil
	}

	if in.Policy.CompositePodGroupData != nil { return fmt.Errorf("leaf workload item %q sets composite building block: policy", item.Name) }
	if in.Constraints.CompositePodGroupData != nil { return fmt.Errorf("leaf workload item %q sets composite building block: constraints", item.Name) }
	if in.DisruptionMode.CompositePodGroupData != nil { return fmt.Errorf("leaf workload item %q sets composite building block: disruptionMode", item.Name) }
	if in.PreemptionPolicy.CompositePodGroupData != nil { return fmt.Errorf("leaf workload item %q sets composite building block: preemptionPolicy", item.Name) }

	return nil
}
```', 'created_at': '2026-07-22T16:02:24+00:00', 'path': 'staging/src/k8s.io/component-helpers/scheduling/schedulingv1/workloadbuilder/builder.go', 'position': 1}, {'user': 'helayoty', 'body': "I'd change it to `userConfig`, wdyt?", 'created_at': '2026-07-22T16:25:05+00:00', 'path': 'staging/src/k8s.io/component-helpers/scheduling/schedulingv1/workloadbuilder/builder.go', 'position': 162}, {'user': 'helayoty', 'body': 'Makes sense. Done.', 'created_at': '2026-07-22T16:31:38+00:00', 'path': 'staging/src/k8s.io/component-helpers/scheduling/schedulingv1/workloadbuilder/builder.go', 'position': 1}, {'user': 'liggitt', 'body': "let's keep as much of the `validate___` code called from Validate() located in validate.go ... it helps clarify when something that impacts API validation is being modified, and maybe we'll eventually be able to scope down API approval to just the validate.go file", 'created_at': '2026-07-22T16:56:58+00:00', 'path': 'staging/src/k8s.io/component-helpers/scheduling/schedulingv1/workloadbuilder/builder.go', 'position': 1}, {'user': 'liggitt', 'body': "This can be a follow-up, but the `PathElements []string` structure for constructing field paths for errors is not sufficient to express item indices. For that you need to call fieldpath.Item(index), not fieldpath.Child(...).

We didn't notice before because our inputs were singular, but for items like this, we're likely to have lists. Since the Validate function already returns field.ErrorList, I think a follow-up should just switch `PathElements []string` to `FieldPath field.Path` directly.", 'created_at': '2026-07-22T17:05:21+00:00', 'path': 'staging/src/k8s.io/component-helpers/scheduling/schedulingv1/workloadbuilder/types.go', 'position': 33}, {'user': 'mm4tt', 'body': "Ah, that's a good point. I think what we actually need is to have a `*field.Path` field within every `WorkloadItem`, rather than passing a single `rootPath *field.Path` argument to `Validate()`.

While a Job has a single root path (e.g., `job.spec.scheduling`), multi-level controllers will likely require separate paths. For instance, in JobSet, we would have `jobset.spec.scheduling` for the top-level CPG WorkloadItem and `jobset.spec.replicatedjobs[i].scheduling` for each child PG WorkloadItem.

@liggitt  - does that make sense, or do you see a better solution? 
", 'created_at': '2026-07-22T17:13:47+00:00', 'path': 'staging/src/k8s.io/component-helpers/scheduling/schedulingv1/workloadbuilder/types.go', 'position': 33}, {'user': 'liggitt', 'body': 'yeah, that makes sense to me... make the constructor responsible for telling you the path to each piece instead of trying to append paths to paths', 'created_at': '2026-07-22T17:17:55+00:00', 'path': 'staging/src/k8s.io/component-helpers/scheduling/schedulingv1/workloadbuilder/types.go', 'position': 33}, {'user': 'helayoty', 'body': 'Updated. PTAL.', 'created_at': '2026-07-22T17:54:24+00:00', 'path': 'staging/src/k8s.io/component-helpers/scheduling/schedulingv1/workloadbuilder/types.go', 'position': 33}, {'user': 'mm4tt', 'body': 'We could also add an example here to make it clear what this is:

> ... In the controller's API. For instance, for a Job this is "spec.scheduling".', 'created_at': '2026-07-22T18:05:53+00:00', 'path': 'staging/src/k8s.io/component-helpers/scheduling/schedulingv1/workloadbuilder/types.go', 'position': 14}, {'user': 'mm4tt', 'body': '> A nil Path reports at the root path.

It is unclear what "root path" refers to here, so I suggest removing this sentence. *field.Path handles a nil receiver gracefully by design (e.g., calling Child("name") on a nil path yields the same result as field.NewPath("name")), so there is no need to document nil behavior here I guess.', 'created_at': '2026-07-22T18:07:46+00:00', 'path': 'staging/src/k8s.io/component-helpers/scheduling/schedulingv1/workloadbuilder/types.go', 'position': 1}, {'user': 'mm4tt', 'body': 'I don't think this `nil` check is strictly necessary. `*field.Path` handles `nil` receivers natively:
 1. `path.String()` returns `<nil>`, which provides a clear indication that a nil path was provided.
 2. `path.Child("name")` behaves identically to `field.NewPath("name")`.


We should be fine omitting this block. Maybe it will even be better because field.NewPath("").Child("name").String() will return "[].name"


So overall, I don't think we need to change this function at all. The old name was also good - appendPathElements. I'd leave it as it - it should work. You can add a test to validate that everything works when workloadItem.Path is nil.', 'created_at': '2026-07-22T18:15:50+00:00', 'path': 'staging/src/k8s.io/component-helpers/scheduling/schedulingv1/workloadbuilder/validate.go', 'position': 1}, {'user': 'mm4tt', 'body': 'We should likely also remove this.  Nil is better than field.NewPath(""), because field.NewPath("").String() returns "[]"', 'created_at': '2026-07-22T18:17:24+00:00', 'path': 'staging/src/k8s.io/component-helpers/scheduling/schedulingv1/workloadbuilder/validate.go', 'position': 1}, {'user': 'mm4tt', 'body': "NIT:

The path argument is the field path of the Job's spec.scheduling. It is used exclusively for validation error reporting, so the controller may pass nil.", 'created_at': '2026-07-22T18:21:47+00:00', 'path': 'pkg/api/job/util.go', 'position': 1}, {'user': 'mm4tt', 'body': 'This should be returning *schedulingv1alpha3.CompositePodGroup type, not CompositePodGroupTemplate', 'created_at': '2026-07-22T19:12:57+00:00', 'path': 'staging/src/k8s.io/component-helpers/scheduling/schedulingv1/workloadbuilder/builder.go', 'position': 1}]

