[{'user': 'mm4tt', 'body': "The fix looks correct to me. 

There are at least two edge cases where a `PodGroup` is popped, but its pods are skipped, causing `scheduleOnePodGroup` to return early without calling `AddAttemptedPodGroupIfNeeded` (which means `lastPoppedEntity` is not cleared and pods from `pendingPodGroupPods` are not flushed):
1. The pod is deleted right after its `PodGroup` is popped from the queue.
2. An assumed pod is re-added to the scheduling queue due to a subtle race condition: if an update event for the pod arrives just after the main scheduling loop calls `AssumePod` and `sched.SchedulingQueue.Done()` (which removes the pod from `inFlightPods`), the event handler might still evaluate `isAssumed=false` (e.g., cache hasn't synced yet) and call `SchedulingQueue.Update(pod)`. Since the pod is no longer in `inFlightPods`, the queue logic falls through and adds the assumed pod back to the active queue (https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/blob/864d210eb44e25768f8ca6840ab3a2e225651c01/pkg/scheduler/backend/queue/scheduling_queue.go#L1385-L1386)

In both scenarios, when the `PodGroup` is popped, `skipPodGroupPodSchedule` skips the pod, and the loop returns early leaving `lastPoppedEntity` stale. If any other pods from this `PodGroup` fail binding concurrently, they will be routed to `pendingPodGroupPods` (since `isLastPoppedEntity` still returns true) and will be stuck there indefinitely because the `PodGroup` is no longer in the queue.

This PR correctly fixes the issue by ensuring we always clear the last popped entity and flush `pendingPodGroupPods` before exiting `scheduleOnePodGroup`.
", 'created_at': '2026-07-15T11:05:54+00:00', 'path': 'pkg/scheduler/schedule_one_podgroup.go', 'position': 4}, {'user': 'mm4tt', 'body': 'To make it clear how an assumed pod can end up in the queue in the real world, it would be great to add a brief comment explaining the race condition here. We could also make the cache/queue initialization follow the realistic chronological order of events to better document how this situation happens in practice. 

Something like this:

```go
			cache := internalcache.New(ctx, nil, true)
			queue := internalqueue.NewTestQueue(ctx, schedFwk.QueueSortFunc())

			cache.AddPodGroup(podGroup)
			queue.AddPodGroup(logger, podGroup)

            cache.AddPodGroupMember(p1)
			// 1. p1 is added to the queue, its PodGroup is popped, and its scheduling cycle succeeds.
			// The pod gets assumed in the cache and proceeds to binding.
			

			// 2. An assumed pod can only re-enter the queue due to a subtle race condition:
			// an update event arrives, the event handler sees `isAssumed=false` and calls `queue.Update()`.
			// Right before `Update()` acquires the lock, the main scheduling loop assumes the pod 
			// and calls `Done()`, removing it from `inFlightPods`. The `Update()` logic then falls 
			// through and adds the now-assumed pod back to the queue.
			assumedP1 := p1.DeepCopy()
			assumedP1.Spec.NodeName = "node1"
			if err := cache.AssumePod(logger, assumedP1); err != nil {
				t.Fatalf("Failed to assume pod: %v", err)
			}
            queue.Update(logger, p1, p1)

			// 3. During the next cycle, the PodGroup is popped again and contains only the assumed pod.
			entity, err := queue.Pop(logger)
			if err != nil {
				t.Fatalf("Failed to pop pod group: %v", err)
			}
```


This is to avoid situation when someone removes this test in the future because the scenario seems unrealistic. ', 'created_at': '2026-07-15T11:32:05+00:00', 'path': 'pkg/scheduler/schedule_one_podgroup_test.go', 'position': 61}]

