commit_messages:
["validation-gen: better check explicit opt-in/out

Rather than ONLY checking the SecondClosest comments, we can check the
type's real comments, too.", 'validation-gen: Add a flag to skip register funcs', 'validation-gen: Nix emit-register-funcs, use a tag

Get rid of flag `--emit-register-funcs` and use a per-pkg tag.

Set `+k8s:validation-gen-scheme-registry=nil` to disable register
function generation for a pkg.', 'validation-gen: make file-tag more expressive

What used to be:
    `// +k8s:validation-gen=TypeMeta`
...is now:
    `// +k8s:validation-gen=TypesWithField=TypeMeta`
...which leaves room for other criteria', 'validation-gen: Add file tag for TypesWithSuffix

This lets me use it more directly in protobuf code where I really only
need to generate validation for Requests, not Responses.', 'validation-gen: Change MatchFunc to take pointers

This clarifies code for upcoming changes but is also a small perf win.

Given this benchmark:

./bm/doc.go:
```
// +k8s:validation-gen=TypesWithField=TypeMeta
// +k8s:validation-gen-scheme-registry=k8s.io/code-generator/cmd/validation-gen/testscheme.Scheme

// This is a test package.
// +k8s:validation-gen-nolint
package bm

import "k8s.io/code-generator/cmd/validation-gen/testscheme"

var localSchemeBuilder = testscheme.New()

type Struct struct {
	 TypeMeta int

	// +k8s:listType=map
	// +k8s:listMapKey=keyField
	ListField []OtherStruct `json:"listField"`
}

type OtherStruct struct {
	KeyField string `json:"keyField"`
	// +k8s:maxLength=1024
	DataField string `json:"dataField"`
}
```

./bm/doc_test.go:
```
package bm

import (
	"context"
	"fmt"
	"strings"
	"testing"

	"k8s.io/apimachinery/pkg/api/operation"
	"k8s.io/apimachinery/pkg/util/validation/field"
)

func BenchmarkSmall(b *testing.B) {
	b.ReportAllocs()
	s := Struct{}
	for i := range 5 {
		s.ListField = append(s.ListField, OtherStruct{
			KeyField:  fmt.Sprintf("key%d", i),
			DataField: strings.Repeat(fmt.Sprintf("data%d", i), 3),
		})
	}
	for i := 0; i < b.N; i++ {
		Validate_Struct(context.Background(), operation.Operation{}, field.NewPath("obj"), &s, nil)
	}
}
func BenchmarkLarge(b *testing.B) {
	b.ReportAllocs()
	s := Struct{}
	for i := range 100 {
		s.ListField = append(s.ListField, OtherStruct{
			KeyField:  fmt.Sprintf("key%d", i),
			DataField: strings.Repeat(fmt.Sprintf("data%d", i), 100),
		})
	}
	for i := 0; i < b.N; i++ {
		Validate_Struct(context.Background(), operation.Operation{}, field.NewPath("obj"), &s, nil)
	}
}
```

The new version is both faster (10-20%) and has less allocs per op (~30%).

before:
BenchmarkSmall-12       43848441               424.5 ns/op           736 B/op         17 allocs/op
BenchmarkLarge-12         750921             24098 ns/op           12896 B/op        302 allocs/op

after:
BenchmarkSmall-12       52705480               336.3 ns/op           576 B/op         12 allocs/op
BenchmarkLarge-12         852565             21278 ns/op            9696 B/op        202 allocs/op', 'validation-gen: Remove now unused DirectEqualPtr', 'validation-gen: Trivial rename for consistency', 'validation-gen: Change MatchFunc signature', 'validation-gen: Add comments', 'validation-gen: Remove 1-use func, rename another', "validation-gen: Support lists of pointers

We don't support this in k8s but it seems that validation-gen can be
used to generate code for other environments, like protobuf, which do
support lists of pointers.

Gemini helped with this one.", 'validation-gen: Rename and comment functions', 'validation-gen: Move functions around, no changes', 'validation-gen: allow other major versions in tag levels', 'Fix lint for ptr.To()', 'Refactor where no-nil-ptrs check lives']

body:
- **Problem:** Verbose titles in custom dialogs could be vertically clipped instead of remaining readable.
- **Change:** Allow the message container and title to shrink, and constrain titles to one line with an ellipsis.

```css
.dialog-message-text {
	min-width: 0;
	overflow: hidden;
	text-overflow: ellipsis;
	white-space: nowrap;
}
```

<!-- START COPILOT CODING AGENT SUFFIX -->

- Fixes #327260

review_comments:
[{'user': 'Copilot', 'body': 'This guard runs after the bracketed-IPv6 and IPv4 early returns, so `user name@127.0.0.1` and `user name@[::1]` are still classified as URLs instead of `unknown`. Move the userinfo-whitespace check before those host-type returns (while retaining the explicit HTTP(S) exception), and add both cases to the new test.', 'created_at': '2026-07-21T18:36:47+00:00', 'path': 'src/vs/workbench/contrib/browserView/common/browserSearch.ts', 'position': 35}, {'user': 'jruales', 'body': "Comment generated by Copilot:

---

Good catch that these reach `url` — but classifying them as `unknown` would actually diverge from Chromium, which is the reference this parser is matching.

In `AutocompleteInput::Parse`, the IPv6/IPv4 host-family returns (lines 440/442) run *before* the `username_has_space → UNKNOWN` check (line 521), and the space-in-host handling is gated on `host_info.family == NEUTRAL`. So Chromium keeps IP-literal hosts as URLs (`user name@127.0.0.1`, `user name@[::1]`) while `user name@example.com` becomes `unknown`.

I've kept the userinfo-whitespace check after the IP returns to preserve that behavior, added a comment explaining the intentional ordering, and added both IP cases to the tests asserting `url` (see 94d0794). Verified with the WHATWG parser too: `new URL('http://user name@127.0.0.1')` → host `127.0.0.1`, user `user%20name`.", 'created_at': '2026-07-21T18:53:03+00:00', 'path': 'src/vs/workbench/contrib/browserView/common/browserSearch.ts', 'position': 35}, {'user': 'Copilot', 'body': 'The new `!isHttpScheme` exception is not exercised: all userinfo-whitespace cases are scheme-less, while all explicit-scheme cases lack userinfo. Add an explicit HTTP case so a future simplification cannot silently remove this documented branch behavior.', 'created_at': '2026-07-21T20:31:13+00:00', 'path': 'src/vs/workbench/contrib/browserView/test/common/browserSearch.test.ts', 'position': 12}, {'user': 'jruales', 'body': "Good call. Added a test that exercises the `!isHttpScheme` exception directly (f5cf41c):

```ts
test('an explicit http(s) scheme keeps whitespace-userinfo input a URL', () => {
    const inputs = [
        'http://user name@example.com',
        'https://user name@example.com',
    ];
    const actual = inputs.map((i) => resolveAddressBarInputType(i));
    assert.deepStrictEqual(actual, inputs.map(() => 'url'));
});
```

These hit the guard with `userinfo` set *and* `isHttpScheme` true, so the branch is now pinned against a future simplification — it sits right next to the scheme-less `user name@example.com` → `unknown` case for contrast. Matches Chromium's explicit-scheme → URL rule (line 491, before the username-space check).", 'created_at': '2026-07-21T20:35:46+00:00', 'path': 'src/vs/workbench/contrib/browserView/test/common/browserSearch.test.ts', 'position': 12}]

body:
@meganrogge Had to revert to unblock our insiders build

From @deepak1556 in Slack
The foundry node addon added in https://github.com/microsoft/vscode/pull/326678 breaks our minimum supported distros, it requires GLIBC 2.34 which requires us to drop Ubuntun 20, Debian 10 and RHEL 8 client support. The onxx runtime itself has satisfying deps, its just the addon.
